<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>ReBooty Ops — SKU Export</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/style.css?v=<%= cssVersion %>">
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <h1>Push SKUs to eBay</h1>
    <%- include('partials/nav') %>

    <div class="card">
      <p>This gives you a simple file mapping each active eBay Item ID to its new
        <strong>RT-####</strong> SKU (<%= count %> items). It's not the actual eBay
        upload file -- eBay's File Exchange format is specific to your account, so
        build the upload from eBay's own download rather than a from-scratch file.</p>

      <p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>
      <ol style="padding-left:1.25rem;">
        <li>In eBay Seller Hub, go to File Exchange &rarr; Download &rarr; download your
          active listings report as a CSV.</li>
        <li>Download the mapping file below.</li>
        <li>In a spreadsheet, use a lookup (VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP on Item ID) to fill the
          downloaded file's <strong>Custom Label</strong> column from the mapping
          file's SKU column.</li>
        <li>Set the <strong>Action</strong> column to <code>Revise</code> for those rows.</li>
        <li>Upload the edited file back through File Exchange &rarr; Upload.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>This only covers Active listings -- Sold/ended items aren't included since
        eBay won't let you revise an ended listing.</p>
    </div>

    <a class="button-secondary" href="/sku-export/download">Download SKU Mapping (CSV)</a>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
